00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.900
This is the Cavendish banana.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.990
Or, for most of the world, just “banana”.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.630
Because this one species accounts for 99%
of exports,

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:21.450
and is bought more than any other item at
the grocery store.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.820
Walmart’s bread and butter isn’t actually
bread or butter, but bananas.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.570
And it’s one of the most important crops
in developing countries, along with staples

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.910
like rice, corn, and wheat.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:40.010
Which makes sense, Bananas are nutritious,
conveniently packaged by nature, and, dirt

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.010
cheap.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.830
In the United States, bananas cost an average
of just 56 cents a pound.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.110
Roughly 19 cents each.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:52.170
Cheap enough that Amazon just gave them away
in Seattle.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.950
And that’s from a company which…

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.590
literally sells air.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:59.960
Oranges, in comparison, cost just over two
dollars a pound.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.040
Strawberries, three thirty-four, and apples,
a dollar sixty-two.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.659
The difference is, bananas are picky.

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:12.820
They require extremely rich soil, protection
from the wind, and lots of water.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:19.600
They can really only grow in this narrow region
- about 30 degrees north or south of the equator.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.790
That eliminates Europe and most of the United
States, give or take a volcano and alligator.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:30.901
So, if you live here, in the banana belt,
a country like Brazil, India, or China, your

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:33.020
bananas are grown locally.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:38.150
For the rest of us, they’re imported from
Central and South America - usually Ecuador,

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.119
Guatemala, or Costa Rica.

00:01:40.119 --> 00:01:42.780
Apples, on the other hand, take what they
can get.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:50.110
They survive in many types of soil, and withstand
both cold winters and warm summers, with not

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.670
one, but thousands of different varieties.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:02:00.750
So, why after being shipped 3, or 4, or 6
thousand miles away are bananas still cheaper

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.759
than apples grown a few blocks away?

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:06.320
The answer is, largely: the container.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.840
The story of the banana and 90% of everything
we buy is that of the container ship, and

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.810
how it forever changed the global economy.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:18.450
But first, let’s rewind.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.209
There are many ways to get goods from one
place to another.

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:26.950
Planes are fast, trucks are precise, and trains
are efficient.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.130
But water is by far the cheapest.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:35.740
There are no roads to build, or intersections
to stop at, and once a ship starts moving,

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.570
it requires very little attention.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:44.220
Some of the biggest container ships in the
world are manned by just 13 crew members.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:49.530
And companies like Rolls-Royce are developing
self-driving versions as we speak.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:55.810
The problem, until recently, was moving cargo
onto and off of the ship, which often took

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.459
even more time than actually sailing.

00:02:58.459 --> 00:03:04.450
That’s because workers had to lift, carry,
and drag everything from barrels, to crates,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.129
and heavy bags.

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:07.129
Not so fun.

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:08.740
And not very fast.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:15.670
A journey from New York to Europe, for example,
might only take 12 days, but loading and unloading

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.349
would take another 7.

00:03:17.349 --> 00:03:21.760
That’s 7 full days it could’ve spent delivering
more cargo.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.000
Therefore, 7 full days added to the cost of
shipping.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.099
Port fees, dock workers, and their equipment
accounted for 60-75% of the cost of shipping.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:38.600
So, by the time a product reached our shelves,
its price would have to be raised an average

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.910
12% to make up for it.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.950
But everything changed with the container.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:49.101
Companies realized they weren’t in the business
of shipping bananas, or cars, or phones, They

00:03:49.101 --> 00:03:51.340
were in the business of… shipping.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:57.200
What’s inside the box shouldn’t matter,
their job is generic, efficient transportation.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.790
In fact, today, crew members don’t even
know most of what they’re carrying.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:06.530
If shipping companies could take everything
and put it in a single size box, they could

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:12.580
turn thousands of individual problems into
just one: How to move a known object from

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.990
one place to another as quickly as possible.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.829
If you’re a longshoreman, this is your worst
nightmare.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:25.490
Your job, and in many cases, whole coastal
economies, were now based on a repetitive,

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:27.879
low-skill, and easily automated task.

00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:30.240
Unions, of course, fought back.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.129
At one point, a workers’ strike lasted 83
days.

00:04:34.129 --> 00:04:37.659
But the universal law is money finds a way.

00:04:37.659 --> 00:04:42.600
One company calculated that shortening the
time required to lift a container by a single

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.979
second would save $4,000 per ship, per year.

00:04:46.979 --> 00:04:52.819
And they wouldn’t be saving seconds, or
minutes, or even hours, but days.

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:57.419
Containers lowered the cost of insurance,
made theft nearly impossible, and allowed

00:04:57.419 --> 00:05:01.520
for goods to be easily moved between ship,
rail, and truck.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.439
The container was inevitable.

00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:06.039
The only question was which one.

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:10.431
Four separate standards organizations, of
course, had… four separate answers, but

00:05:10.431 --> 00:05:14.779
we’ll fast forward years of fascinating
debate and just say,

00:05:14.779 --> 00:05:22.080
The world decided on these iconic 20 and 40
feet boxes you now see at every port in every

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.080
country.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:28.219
Today, ports are filled not with workers,
but giant cranes which move containers in

00:05:28.219 --> 00:05:31.259
and out of ships in the most efficient order
possible.

00:05:31.259 --> 00:05:33.889
And ships spend most of their time in motion.

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:36.910
The challenge is keeping containers full.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:42.529
The ideal route goes from A to B, where it
loads new cargo for its return back to A.

00:05:42.529 --> 00:05:44.689
But this isn’t always possible.

00:05:44.689 --> 00:05:49.399
China, for example, has both high exports
and imports.

00:05:49.399 --> 00:05:53.389
Problem is, its manufactured goods are sent
out in container ships.

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:58.419
But the raw materials it imports to make them
arrive in bulk cargo ships.

00:05:58.419 --> 00:06:04.699
As a result, roughly 60% of containers shipped
from Asia to North America return empty.

00:06:04.699 --> 00:06:06.710
Australia is the opposite.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:12.319
It exports raw goods like iron, gold, and
coal, but imports its products on container

00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:13.319
ships.

00:06:13.319 --> 00:06:18.429
Cruise ships survive by offering repositioning
trips - cheaper fares for longer, one-way

00:06:18.429 --> 00:06:21.300
routes it uses to change regions.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.779
Container ships don’t have that luxury,
But their schedules are so predictable that

00:06:25.779 --> 00:06:30.839
factories can manufacture goods as they need
them, rather than storing large amounts of

00:06:30.839 --> 00:06:31.899
inventory.

00:06:31.899 --> 00:06:34.470
In other words, everything is cheap.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.030
And there’s a lot of it.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:39.430
Shipping costs are so low that they almost
don’t matter.

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.490
The only thing that does is labor.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:47.449
It’s this combination of low wages, trivial
shipping costs, and tight planning which turn

00:06:47.449 --> 00:06:50.899
the banana into the perfect consumer product.

00:06:50.899 --> 00:06:55.119
It thrives not despite its limitations but
because of them.

00:06:55.119 --> 00:07:00.149
The journey begins in Ecuador, the world’s
number one banana exporter.

00:07:00.149 --> 00:07:05.379
Thousands of plantations produce 2 billion
dollars worth of the fruit every year.

00:07:05.379 --> 00:07:09.589
Bananas are picked while still green, in the
first of seven stages.

00:07:09.589 --> 00:07:12.699
And immediately, the clock starts ticking.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:16.819
Because once a banana begins to ripen, the
process is irreversible.

00:07:16.819 --> 00:07:21.759
Worse, a single banana ripening will trigger
all those around it.

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:26.110
So, everything that happens next is carefully
planned to prevent that.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:30.340
First, imperfect bananas are sold locally
or fed to livestock.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:35.069
The rest are packed in special containers
called reefers, which keep them at a controlled

00:07:35.069 --> 00:07:38.619
55.94 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:07:38.619 --> 00:07:43.429
Before being loaded, reefers are sealed, so
they won’t be exposed to open air until

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:44.610
they reach their destination.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:49.889
Next, they’re transported, usually by truck,
to the dock, where they’re loaded 40 or

00:07:49.889 --> 00:07:51.379
50 an hour onto ships.

00:07:51.379 --> 00:07:58.929
A large boat can carry 80 million bananas,
a whole year’s supply for 600,000 Americans.

00:07:58.929 --> 00:08:05.899
And by traveling at only 18 or 19 knots, they
save millions on their biggest expense: fuel.

00:08:05.899 --> 00:08:12.300
Ships may burn 400 tons of bunker fuel per
day, which at current prices, amounts to nearly

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.990
$200,000 in a single 24 hour period.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:22.619
Our ship, the Dole Pacific, makes deliveries
from Ecuador to San Diego all year round.

00:08:22.619 --> 00:08:27.090
By the time it arrives, bananas have reached
stage 2 - light green.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.520
They’re then moved to ripening rooms, where
they’re sprayed with ethylene gas to trigger

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.329
their ripening process.

00:08:33.329 --> 00:08:37.510
Once they reach stage 3, they’re ready for
delivery to the grocery store.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:42.860
During this trip, they transition from 3 to
4, to 5, when they’re finally bought for

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.520
less than a quarter.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:50.870
With a single, uniform variety, grown where
labor is cheap, and sold in high-value consumer

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:55.950
markets, bananas perfectly represent the efficiency
of global shipping.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:09:01.259
The beauty of container ships is their ability
to conveniently deliver goods regardless of

00:09:01.259 --> 00:09:02.259
where.

